<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Landshut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Landshut is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isar">Isar</a>, and the seat of the region's government. Founded in 1204 by Duke Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a>, it was the residence of the dukes of Lower Bavaria and from 1392 to 1503 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>. The Landshut Wedding of 1475 is re-enacted every four years, and St Martin's Church has the tallest brick tower in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/landshut.jpg" alt="Landshut" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H.Helmlechner · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landshut_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Landshut lies in the Alpine foothills, about 70 kilometres from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and Regensburg. The River Isar runs through the city and splits in two in the centre, leaving the Mühleninsel between its arms. The Hofberg, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a> stands, is the highest point in the city area, at 505 metres above sea level.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A settlement grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a> after its foundation in 1204. Landshut was a Wittelsbach residence by 1231, and in 1255, when the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was divided in two, it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>. Duke Henry XVI was the first of the three 'rich dukes' who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> in the 15th century. In 1475 the wedding of Duke George with the Polish princess Jadwiga Jagiellon was held in the town with one of the most splendid festivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. After his death and the Landshut War of Succession, Bavaria-Landshut was reunited with Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><p>Between 1537 and 1543 Louis X, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, built the Landshut Residence after a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace constructed north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and was modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-te">Palazzo Te</a> in Mantua. William V, Duke of Bavaria, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a> from a Gothic fortification into a Renaissance complex while he lived in Landshut as crown prince for ten years until 1579. The town then lost most of its importance until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-ingolstadt">University of Ingolstadt</a> was moved there in 1800; in 1826 the university was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><p>Swedish forces took and plundered the city three times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, in 1632, 1634 and 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> won the Battle of Landshut in 1809 against an Austrian army, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a subcamp of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau concentration camp</a>, supplying slave labour for local industry, stood in the city, which was taken by American troops on 29 April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Landshut has a population of more than 75,000 and is the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>, as well as the seat of the surrounding district. Its historic centre is mostly Gothic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a>, the Church of Saint Martin, the Churches of St. Jodok and Holy Spirit, the Town Hall and the Ländtor, the only surviving gate of the medieval fortification. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> courtyard of Trausnitz and the ducal Residence stand beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> churches. The Landshuter Hochzeit, held every four years, commemorates the marriage of 1475.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Landshut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the later Middle Ages towns such as Munich, founded by Henry the Lion in 1158, <b>Landshut</b> and Ingolstadt grew under ducal protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He moved the university from <b>Landshut</b> to Munich in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was constantly at war with his cousins in <b>Landshut</b> and Munich and was placed under the imperial ban.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-ingolstadt">Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Landshut</b>, seat of the Lower Bavarian dukes since the 13th century, became capital of the wealthiest share, which gained part of the Duchy of Bavaria-Straubing in 1429 and all of Ingolstadt in 1447.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote>In the partition of the Duchy of Bavaria in 1255, Henry XIII received Lower Bavaria, the lands along the lower Isar, the Danube and the Inn, with <b>Landshut</b> as residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lower-bavaria">Duchy of Lower Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
